WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:04.160
Film Courage: West, when you first start writing
a stage play or a screenplay (even for a short

00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:07.310
film or a feature film), is there a structure
that you follow?

00:00:07.310 --> 00:00:08.310
Do you start outlining?

00:00:08.310 --> 00:00:09.610
What’s your process?

00:00:09.610 --> 00:00:13.030
Let’s say you have an idea, you have a story
you want to tell.

00:00:13.030 --> 00:00:17.060
What are some of the things you first do in
the beginning?

00:00:17.060 --> 00:00:21.109
West Liang: For me, I let it sit for awhile.

00:00:21.109 --> 00:00:23.050
You know, I let it sit for awhile.

00:00:23.050 --> 00:00:30.009
And I tend to…what I’ve learned over the
years from my own personal kind of instrument

00:00:30.009 --> 00:00:35.950
is that when I first started I would get excited
about an idea and I would tell everyone about

00:00:35.950 --> 00:00:36.950
it.

00:00:36.950 --> 00:00:41.640
I would call my brother, I would tell my friend,
I would call you and tell you, I would call

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:43.880
him and tell him.

00:00:43.880 --> 00:00:48.010
And then (because you’re excited about it
and I think that is a good energy to have

00:00:48.010 --> 00:00:52.800
when you have a story to tell), but what I
do now is I don’t tell anyone about it.

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:58.510
And I think that is important for me because
I think you want to…it’s almost like a

00:00:58.510 --> 00:00:59.730
boxer.

00:00:59.730 --> 00:01:04.600
You want to get into the ring with a much
energy as possible so that when the bell rings,

00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:08.360
you’re coming out swinging!

00:01:08.360 --> 00:01:13.140
And what I’ve discovered over the years
is that when you spend all your energy telling

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:18.030
people about it and then you get in front
of the computer, you have no energy.

00:01:18.030 --> 00:01:20.740
You’re done telling people about it, right?

00:01:20.740 --> 00:01:23.690
So for me, I keep it all bagged up.

00:01:23.690 --> 00:01:30.240
I get excited about it and I talk to myself
about it and I think about it a whole lot,

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:35.969
and I get it to the point where it’s about
70 percent done.

00:01:35.969 --> 00:01:40.039
I never want to get to the point where it’s
100 percent done because you want to allow

00:01:40.039 --> 00:01:42.509
for more inspiration and changes.

00:01:42.509 --> 00:01:47.060
So for me I get it to the point where it’s
about 70 percent figured out and then there

00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:53.579
is all this kind of energy boiling and then
I wake up really early in the morning, have

00:01:53.579 --> 00:02:00.570
my coffee and I spend all that energy getting
it out on the page.

00:02:00.570 --> 00:02:05.680
And then it’s on the page and then you have
a little bit of kind of security, and then

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:11.480
maybe you can tell people about one scene
or whatever.

00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:14.920
I think it’s great to keep energy wrapped
up.

00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:19.650
Film Courage: That’s interesting because
I think that there’s a lot of people that

00:02:19.650 --> 00:02:21.129
don’t [keep their ideas under wraps] when
they first come here [to the Los Angeles area].

00:02:21.129 --> 00:02:22.129
I’m just wondering, were you always like
this?

00:02:22.129 --> 00:02:23.129
Was there a turning point?

00:02:23.129 --> 00:02:26.489
Was there like something that happened where
you said “no more?”

00:02:26.489 --> 00:02:28.040
West Liang: I think trial and error.

00:02:28.040 --> 00:02:30.540
I think that’s what it’s like for everybody.

00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:33.650
What works for me may not work for other people.

00:02:33.650 --> 00:02:35.140
I’m a morning person.

00:02:35.140 --> 00:02:37.189
I like to write during the day.

00:02:37.189 --> 00:02:40.040
I know what my habits are when I get into
a writing block.

00:02:40.040 --> 00:02:41.720
I know what my habits are when I get into
a writing block.

00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:46.849
When I get into a writing block, I almost
always go hiking by myself.

00:02:46.849 --> 00:02:52.580
I almost always go and watch movies by myself.

00:02:52.580 --> 00:02:55.740
And again, other people have different things.

00:02:55.740 --> 00:03:01.090
For me, I’ve gotten to know my kind of “instrument”
over the years.

00:03:01.090 --> 00:03:07.269
I’ve gotten to know how I like to work and
how I like to solve problems.

00:03:07.269 --> 00:03:12.450
So for me, not telling people about the story
works.

00:03:12.450 --> 00:03:15.750
Film Courage: How do you know you’re in
a [writing] block?

00:03:15.750 --> 00:03:19.690
I know that sounds like a really strange question
because it sounds like it would be so obvious…but

00:03:19.690 --> 00:03:22.880
maybe you’re not in a block?

00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:28.431
How do you know you’re not simply tired
or something else is going on, but it’s

00:03:28.431 --> 00:03:29.860
an actual block with the story?

00:03:29.860 --> 00:03:32.069
West Liang: That’s a really good question.

00:03:32.069 --> 00:03:36.840
I do think that sometimes you do get tired.

00:03:36.840 --> 00:03:49.439
I think for me…this sounds really kind of
weird but…and I’m sure a lot of the writers

00:03:49.439 --> 00:03:54.960
who might be watching this might understand
because this is such an esoteric, kind of

00:03:54.960 --> 00:03:56.689
writerly, bullshit…

00:03:56.689 --> 00:04:00.270
Film Courage: I’m really good at that [laughs]!

00:04:00.270 --> 00:04:02.200
West Liang: [Laughs] You know?

00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:09.379
But I think if the writer is too much in the
driver’s seat, that’s a bad thing.

00:04:09.379 --> 00:04:15.609
I think at some point the story has a life
of its own.

00:04:15.609 --> 00:04:27.960
And I think in some ways a writer has to sort
of surrender to the story, to the characters.

00:04:27.960 --> 00:04:34.150
Some of the work…I wrote this really successful
play.

00:04:34.150 --> 00:04:39.410
It was produced in 2012.

00:04:39.410 --> 00:04:43.110
It was really well-received and I’m incredibly
proud of it.

00:04:43.110 --> 00:04:44.472
That script came out of me in like two weeks.

00:04:44.472 --> 00:04:54.360
And the idea came to me at 3 in the morning
in a flash.

00:04:54.360 --> 00:05:02.169
And so there is something kind of spiritual
and metaphysical about that which I try not

00:05:02.169 --> 00:05:03.880
to answer and figure out.

00:05:03.880 --> 00:05:04.889
Film Courage: Yes.

00:05:04.889 --> 00:05:05.889
That’s fair.

00:05:05.889 --> 00:05:12.870
West Liang: So I think when you start to get
too formulaic, when you start to try and shoehorn

00:05:12.870 --> 00:05:19.640
too many things together, when you feel yourself
b*llsh*tting…it’s weird.

00:05:19.640 --> 00:05:28.460
It’s a fine line between being authentic,
being inspired and being disciplined.

00:05:28.460 --> 00:05:30.940
Those are really kind of weird intersections.

00:05:30.940 --> 00:05:34.800
Film Courage: So in other words, it sounds
like you don’t really want to overthink

00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:37.810
it too much and have a name for the process.

00:05:37.810 --> 00:05:39.139
You just know it’s instinctual.

00:05:39.139 --> 00:05:40.139
West Liang: Yeah.

00:05:40.139 --> 00:05:41.160
I try to.

00:05:41.160 --> 00:05:42.960
It’s a balance.

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:46.180
We keep on going back to the word balance.

00:05:46.180 --> 00:05:54.270
Because I tend to be very heady sometimes,
but you don’t want to go overboard.

00:05:54.270 --> 00:05:59.139
Film Courage: Which I don’t think is a bad
thing, because it keep you on track.

00:05:59.139 --> 00:06:03.190
Because if you go too far out, you might be
too much of a dreamer and all these ideas

00:06:03.190 --> 00:06:07.759
are there and you’re telling everybody,
but nothing is happening.

00:06:07.759 --> 00:06:12.229
Whereas, when you talked about it earlier,
“No 90 pages is not going to work.

00:06:12.229 --> 00:06:16.149
It’s got to be 75 pages because it’s going
to cost this [too much for our budget].”

00:06:16.149 --> 00:06:17.149
That sounds like it’s working.

00:06:17.149 --> 00:06:22.460
West Liang: I think ALL I WANT (the project)
was unique because it’s a collaborative

00:06:22.460 --> 00:06:29.699
project with another person (actress Melissa
Center) who is very personally tied to the

00:06:29.699 --> 00:06:37.160
story in terms of her heart strings.

00:06:37.160 --> 00:06:45.190
So I didn't have the authority to make all
of the decisions in a vacuum, for better or

00:06:45.190 --> 00:06:46.220
worse.

00:06:46.220 --> 00:06:53.659
We had to really have conversations about
character and plot all along the way.

00:06:53.659 --> 00:07:00.159
Which is different from previous projects
in the sense that I kind of wrote it on my

00:07:00.159 --> 00:07:07.550
own and I kind of had an idea of what I wanted
to accomplish.

